    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "71dd999f_4c4def8a",
        "filename": "util/ectool.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 152,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000253
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-09-01T22:05:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Since a chip may control multiple ports, Should we modify this to take both a chip and a port? ie, \"pdcontrol 0 0 suspend\" will suspend port 0 of chip 0.\n\nIt\u0027s probably more burdensome for most use cases, but seems like it will mitigate the risk of needing changes down the road.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 152,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 152,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "dfc83791f814aa20b1b59315d771bb9432c56e74",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "71dd999f_0c3f77c5",
        "filename": "util/ectool.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 152,
      "author": {
        "id": 1001696
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-09-01T22:11:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If we were starting from scratch, I would just take a port parameter, and let the EC map port-to-chip on its side. But I don\u0027t think we should change it at this point (maybe others disagree), so basically we\u0027re stuck with a solution that requires the host command sender to know the port-to-chip mapping. It\u0027s slightly inconvenient.",
      "parentUuid": "71dd999f_4c4def8a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 152,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 152,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "dfc83791f814aa20b1b59315d771bb9432c56e74",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "71dd999f_8f219963",
        "filename": "util/ectool.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 152,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000253
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-09-01T23:14:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I really like that idea from a syntax standpoint. Is there already an easy way to map port to chip? I was thinking adding a function in board.c to do it.\n\nFor most boards it would be a trivial function - \"return 0\" if the board has 1 TCPC or \"return port\" if each TCPC controls a single port. If we have a board where TCPCs control multiple ports things might get a bit more complicated, but not by much.\n\nYea or Nay?",
      "parentUuid": "71dd999f_0c3f77c5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 152,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 152,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "dfc83791f814aa20b1b59315d771bb9432c56e74",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "71dd999f_3dcc27b2",
        "filename": "util/ectool.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 152,
